==English==
{{was wotd|2008|February|1}}

===Etymology===
{{coin|en|H. L. Mencken}} from {{m|en|ecdysis|gloss=moulting}} (on the model of {{m|en|enthusiast}} etc.).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɛkˈdɪz.i.æst/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-ecdysiast.ogg|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|uncommon}} An [[erotic]] [[dancer]] who removes their [[clothes]] as a form of [[entertainment]]; a [[stripper]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1973|author=Kyril Bonfiglioli|title=Don't Point That Thing at Me|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2001|page=79|text=I had never seen an '''ecdysiast''' before; toward the end she was wearing nothing but seven beads, four of them sweat.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|author=Chrysti the Wordsmith|title=Verbivore's Feast: A Banquet of Word and Phrase Origins|publisher=Farcountry Press|page=107|text=However, the Queen of '''Ecdysiasts''', Gypsy Rose Lee, was not amused. In a 1940 interview, she leveled her guns against Mencken: "'''Ecdysiast''', he calls me! Why, the man... has been reading books! Dictionaries! We don't wear feathers and molt them off... What does he know about stripping?"}}

